{"type":"npc","name":"Tanglejack","folder":null,"system":{"attributes":{"hp":{"value":135,"temp":0,"max":135,"details":""},"speed":{"value":30,"otherSpeeds":[],"details":""},"ac":{"value":26,"details":""},"allSaves":{"value":""}},"initiative":{"statistic":"perception"},"details":{"languages":{"value":["aklo","fey","sakvroth"],"details":""},"level":{"value":8},"blurb":"","publicNotes":"<p>The constructs that would come to be known as cranklejacks were built long ago by a conclave of nymphs to protect the beautiful places of the world. Unfortunately, gremlins love to tinker, break, and corrupt, and over the centuries, they managed to reimagine these once-noble protectors in their own image. Dubbed cranklejacks, the resulting constructs are unreliable, mercurial, and random, interfering with the use of magic around them while barely able to predict the results of their own attacks.</p><p>Still, cranklejacks don't have the full malevolence of a true gremlin, and, as they are intelligent, it can be possible to appeal to their reason. Even so, they have no desire to fulfill responsibilities as guardians any longer, and even those who don't follow the gremlins' path of destruction are grateful to gremlins for freeing them from that yoke.</p><h2>Tanglejacks</h2><p>Either through a quirk in their creation or a conscious effort to return to their roots, some cranklejacks possess a stronger connection to the natural world than the rest of their kin. These so-called \"tanglejacks\" replace their hammer Strike with a sickle that deals 2d4 + 11 slashing damage and has the agile and trip traits and their impaling spike Strike with a vine that deals 2d8 + 11 bludgeoning damage and has a reach of 15 feet. Some tanglejacks might also substitute one or more of their innate spells with spells of equal or lower rank chosen from the primal spell list.</p>","privateNotes":"","publication":{"title":"Battlezoo Bestiary: Elemental Storm (Pathfinder)","authors":"RFC Community","license":"ORC"